# Data scripts and migrations are applied to D1 with `wrangler d1 execute --file`.
# A CRLF checkout changes the bytes that reach the database: the three classes
# imported in #92 landed in production with 
 inside their stored markdown
# because git rewrote the scripts on the post-merge checkout. Pin LF so the
# applied file is always byte-identical to the reviewed one.
*.sql text eol=lf

# Vendored minified libraries are committed byte-exact so the SRI hash
# published for the version stays verifiable against the checkout. No
# line-ending normalization.
apps/filament-forge/vendor/* -text

# Self-hosted webfonts. Git detects these as binary on its own - woff2 is full
# of NUL bytes - but the whole file is a checksummed container, so the one
# outcome worth ruling out explicitly is a future core.autocrlf setting deciding
# otherwise. Same reasoning as the vendored libraries above.
shared/fonts/*.woff2 -text

# Workflow YAML. Actions writes each `run:` step to a script and executes it
# with bash, where a trailing CR would fail as `$'\r': command not found`.
#
# THAT CANNOT HAPPEN TODAY AND THIS RULE IS NOT FIXING A LIVE FAULT - measured
# 2026-09-03, not reasoned about. Git already stores LF in the index for every
# text file here (`core.autocrlf` is true on this machine, so the CRLF is a
# working-tree artifact and the blobs are LF), and a Linux runner checks out
# what is stored. The risk this rules out is a FUTURE `core.autocrlf` or
# attribute change deciding otherwise on some other machine - exactly the
# reasoning the woff2 rule above states for itself, and unlike the .sql rule at
# the top, which was written after an outage rather than before one.
#
# It also keeps this one file LF in the working tree, so what is edited and run
# locally is byte-identical to what the runner executes. REPO-AUDIT.md G8.
.github/workflows/*.yml text eol=lf
